 {
  "id": "honesto",
  "cena": "honesto",
  "et": "The honest part",
  "falas": [
   {
    "txt": "Now, the honest part. Because a sentence is not a spell.",
    "v": {
     "n": 0
    },
    "dest": [
     "not",
     "spell"
    ],
    "p": 0
   },
   {
    "txt": "When researchers filmed fifty-three families at home, the toddlers who heard more praise for effort were, five years later, more likely to believe they could get better at things. That's real.",
    "v": {
     "n": 1
    },
    "dest": [
     "real"
    ],
    "p": 0
   },
   {
    "txt": "But in that same study, being praised as a person didn't predict the opposite. The damage wasn't automatic.",
    "v": {
     "n": 1,
     "x": 1
    },
    "dest": [
     "automatic"
    ],
    "p": 0
   },
   {
    "txt": "And in twenty nineteen, a team in Edinburgh repeated the original experiment with over six hundred children. They didn't find the effect.",
    "v": {
     "n": 2,
     "x": 1
    },
    "dest": [
     "didn't",
     "find"
    ],
    "p": 0
   },
   {
    "txt": "Even Carol Dweck has said they may have put too much emphasis on sheer effort. Telling a kid \"good try\" when nothing is being learned is just a nicer empty sentence.",
    "v": {
     "n": 3,
     "x": 1
    },
    "dest": [
     "empty"
    ],
    "p": 0
   },
   {
    "txt": "So this isn't about banning a word. Saying \"you're smart\" once won't break anyone.",
    "v": {
     "rod": "ONE “YOU'RE SMART” WON'T BREAK ANYONE."
    },
    "dest": [
     "once"
    ],
    "p": 0
   },
   {
    "txt": "It's about what a kid hears a thousand times, and what they come to believe a failure says about them.",
    "v": {
     "rod": "IT IS WHAT A KID HEARS A THOUSAND TIMES."
    },
    "dest": [
     "thousand",
     "times"
    ],
    "p": 0
   }
  ]
 },
